WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.062
Our running example is a small synthetic healthcare operations database.

00:00:04.062 --> 00:00:09.957
The patients table contains a medical record number, name, age, gender, and active status.

00:00:09.957 --> 00:00:10.292
Doctors contains a name and specialty.

00:00:10.292 --> 00:00:14.842
Admissions connects patients and doctors with diagnosis and admission information.

00:00:14.842 --> 00:00:19.142
Clinical notes stores some semi-structured information using PostgreSQL JSONB.

00:00:19.142 --> 00:00:24.277
The data is intentionally small so you can inspect the entire result and understand why a query returns

00:00:24.277 --> 00:00:29.342
particular rows. For teaching simplicity, the patients table stores age directly.

00:00:29.342 --> 00:00:33.632
In a real system, age is often calculated from a birth date for a particular reference date.

00:00:33.632 --> 00:00:39.132
As you work through the module, use these tables to practice the SQL idea rather than focusing on the

00:00:39.132 --> 00:00:40.992
healthcare domain itself.

00:00:40.992 --> 00:00:46.222
Connect this idea to an analyst exploring operational records before building a report.

00:00:46.222 --> 00:00:51.342
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:56.407
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:56.407 --> 00:01:00.127
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:00.127 --> 00:01:04.860
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
